      "maxPoints": 1,
      "modelAnswer": "The rise of Nazi nationalism and Germany 'born anew' under Hitler (Document 1) was a cause that led Germany to wage aggressive war and commit war crimes across Europe—for which its leaders were prosecuted at the Nuremberg Trials (Document 2).",
      "explanation": "Full credit (1 point) for identifying and explaining a valid cause-and-effect link using both documents. Acceptable responses (NYSED rating guide) include: Ley says Germany was born anew under the Nazis, which led to Germany nearly dominating Europe; Nazi nationalism led to war crimes in WWII; Germany rose to power, committed war crimes, and its leaders were put on trial; the Führer promised hope after WWI, so Germany expanded in violation of treaties; German leaders were prosecuted at Nuremberg after the Nazis rose and tried to dominate Europe; Nazi success led German armies to invade Poland and other places; fascist leaders manipulated their people and were later put on trial; or Germany was transformed, which incited them to go to war. Not acceptable: 'Germany was born anew,' 'Hitler stood trial at Nuremberg,' 'Germany was transformed,' 'Hitler rose to power,' 'WWII led to WWI,' 'both reference Germany's defeat,' 'Doc 1 is about the Führer and Doc 2 about war-crimes trials,' 'German armies invaded Europe,' 'both are about Germany,' 'Germans were put on trial if they did not obey the Nazis,' or 'violation of treaties threatened the peace.'",
